# Read by ripgrep in addition to VCS ignore files, and — unlike them — still
# honoured under `--no-ignore-vcs`, which ripgreprc turns on globally. That
# split is the point: VCS ignore hides whole nested repos from a search rooted
# above them, while these are paths that should stay out of every search.
# ripgreprc holds flags, this file holds paths, and neither does the other's
# job.
#
# Generic entries only. Anything true of one workspace or repo belongs in its
# own `.ignore`, which ripgrep reads from ancestors of the search path.

# Noise: directories nobody ever wants to read.
node_modules/

# Worktree checkouts are excluded per repo instead, in that repo's own .ignore:
# a multi-component pattern here does not match from above a repo root, while a
# basename one broad enough to work would hide directories nobody meant to hide.
.venv/
__pycache__/
dist/
build/
.next/
.nuxt/
.turbo/
.svelte-kit/
coverage/
.vercel/
*.lock
pnpm-lock.yaml
package-lock.json

# Secrets, which are here for a different reason than noise. Searching a file
# READS it: `rg SOME_KEY` lands on the line that assigns SOME_KEY, and the
# value is in the transcript before anyone chose to open anything. "Don't read
# files that may hold secrets" cannot cover that, because nobody was reading —
# so ignoring them is what makes that rule hold, and these entries are a safety
# mechanism rather than a convenience.
#
# `--no-ignore` reaches the real file again, and is the deliberate way in when
# the file itself is the point — but ALWAYS pair it with `-l`. `rg --no-ignore
# -l NAME` says which file holds a name; without `-l` it prints the assignment,
# value and all, which is the failure this block exists to prevent and has
# already happened once with a live key.
#
# Narrow on purpose. `.mise.toml` is tracked, shared, holds no secrets, and is
# the first thing anyone greps to learn what a repo configures, so the tempting
# `*mise*.toml` is wrong — only the `local` forms hold values.
.env
.env.*
.mise*.local.toml
mise*.local.toml
*secrets*.env
*credentials.json
*.pem
*.key

# Ignoring a file costs discovery: `rg SOME_KEY` no longer finds where SOME_KEY
# is declared, which is how anyone learns it exists at all. A tracked
# `<filename>.example` beside each ignored file pays that back. That is a
# requirement rather than a nicety — the pair only works if both halves exist —
# and the rules live here rather than in `agents/AGENTS.md`, which every
# session loads and which should not carry mechanics that fire this rarely:
#
#   * Name it by appending `.example` to the real filename —
#     `.mise.local.toml.example`, `.env.example`. The negation below matches
#     exactly that shape, so a differently-named template is itself unsearchable.
#   * It carries EVERY variable name the real file holds, with an empty value,
#     plus enough comment to say what each is for, where its value comes from,
#     and which are secret. The names are the payload: they are what a search
#     finds.
#   * It carries NO value — not a real one, not a truncated or redacted one,
#     and not a placeholder shaped like the real thing. It is tracked, so
#     anything in it is published.
#   * A variable added to the real file is added here in the same change. A
#     name missing from the example is invisible to everyone who does not
#     already know it exists, which is the state this prevents.
#   * What is required is that every name is findable ONCE, not that every
#     ignored file has a twin. A generated or pulled file (`vercel env pull`
#     output) mirrors an authored one and needs no example of its own.
#
# This line is last because a later pattern overrides an earlier one.
!*.example
